## Acute Stroke

## Bradycardia

- **Definition** - rhythm disorder with heart rate less than 60bpm, but for assessment and management of patient w/ symptomatic bradycardia, it is typically defined as having a heart rate of \< 50 bpm
- **Rhythms of bradycardia:**
  - Sinus bradycardia
  - 1st-degree AV block - reduced conduction velocity resulting in prolonged PQ interval with no dropped beat
  - 2nd-degree AV block - reduced conduction velocity resulting in some blocked conduction:
    - Mobitz type I AV block (Wenkebach phenomenon) - successive prolongation of the PR interval culminating into a dropped ventricular conduction, with the cycle of progressive lengthening of PR interval repeating after failure of conduction of the atrial impulse
    - Mobitz type II AV block - intermittent non-conduction of P waves with constant PR interval or conducted beat
  - 3rd-degree AV block - complete AV dissociation
  - Escape rhythms
- **Assessment of symptomatic bradycardia** - unstable bradycardia exists clinically when the following 3 criteria is met:
  - <u>Inappropriately low heart rate</u> - heart rate is slow for a given context (e.g. relative bradycardia of 70bpm is insufficient for a patient with shock)
  - <u>Symptomatic</u> - the patients have S/S that suggest low CO, including 1) Hypotension, 2) Altered mental status, 3) Signs of shock, 4) Ischaemic chest discomfort, 5) Acute pulmonary oedema
- **Adult Bradycardia Algorithm** - steps for assessing the Mx of patient presenting w/ unstable bradycardia w/ a pulse: 
![](img/acls_adult_bradycardia_algorithm.png)
  - **Identification of bradycardia:**
    - HR \< 50 bpm
    - Inadequate or inapropriate for patient's condition (relative bradycardia)
    - Attributable for S/S
  - **Identification and Tx of reversible causes** - O2, IV access, cardiac monitoring:
    - <u>O2 therapy</u> - bradycardia can be a sign of life-threatening hypoxia
    - <u>IV access</u> - establish IV access for medications
    - <u>Establish monitoring</u> - cardiac monitoring (BP/P, rhythm), pulse oximetry (SpO2). += 12-lead ECG if available
    - <u>Primary assessment</u>:
      - **Airway** - maintain patent airway (check if patient can speak in words)
      - **Breathing** - monitor SpO2, give O2 if hypoxia, assist breathing as necessary
      - **Circulation** - cardiac monitor for BP/P, SpO2; obtain and review 12-lead ECG; establish IV access
      - **Disability and exposure** - focused P/E to look for reversible causes (e.g. hypoxia, toxicology causes)
  - **Identification of consequences of bradycardia** - S/S may be caused by, or reflect cause of bradycardia:
    - <u>Symptoms</u> - delirium, ischaemic chest discomfort, SOB
    - <u>Signs</u> - Acute heart failure (basal creps), hypotension, signs of shock (hypoperfusion)
  - **Determine the need to correct bradycardia:**
    - <u>Adequate perfusion</u> (completely asymptomatic) - monitor and observe
    - <u>Poor perfusion</u> (asymptomatic as above) - commence pharmacological and non-pharmacological Tx
  - **Treatment sequence:**
    - <u>1st line Tx</u> - 1mg atropine (max 3mg)
    - <u>Escalation of Tx</u>:
      - Transcutaneous pacing +/- dopamine or epinephrine infusion
      - Expert consultation and transvenous pacing
- **Drugs used in Tx of bradycardia:**
  - **Atropine** - indicated for symptomatic bradycardia with no immediate cause:
    - <u>Dosing</u> - 1mg bolus IV push (except heart transplant patients), repeat every 3-5 min
    - <u>Maximum dose</u> - 3mg (i.e. 3x push)
    - <u>MOA</u> - parasympathetic antagonism to increase HR and conduction velocity of AV node (no effect on infranodal tissue conduction)
    - <u>Caution</u>:
      - 1\. Low dose atropine (\< 0.5 mg IV) may have -ve chronotropic effects
      - 2\. Caution use in presence of myocardial ischaemia or MI as reflex tachycardia may result in increased myocardial demand and worsen ischaemia
      - 3\. Limited utility if in 2nd or 3rd-degree AV block with wide QRS complex, as pathology is found in infranodal tissue
      - 4\. Atropine administration should not delay TCP or beta-adrenergic infusion especially for patients with impending cardiac arrest
      - 5\. No role of atropine in overdose of rate limiting agents (BBs, CCBs), and are often skipped
    - <u>Complications</u>:
      - VF precipitation for unstable heart transplant patients (CLASS III evidence in ECS guidelines)
      - Worsening myocardial ischaemi
  - **Dopamine** (infusion):
    - <u>MOA</u> - adrenergic activity at different doses:
      - **Beta-1 adrenergic activity at lower doses** (\< 10 mcg/kg/min) - more selective inotropic effect
      - **Alpha-1 adrenergic activity at higher doses** (\> 10 mcg/kg/min) - causes vasoconstriction and increased afterload
    - <u>Dosing</u> - 5-20 mcg/kg/min titrated to patient response
  - **Adrenaline** (infusion):
    - <u>MOA</u> - adrenergic activity:
      - Beta-1 adrenergic activity - inotropic effect
      - Alpha-1 adrenergic activity - vasoconstrictive effect
- **Transcutaneous pacing** (TCP):
  - <u>Principles</u> - device that delivers electrical stimulus cutaneously to pace the heart
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - Haemodynamically unstable bradycardia - e.g. hypotension, acute altered mental status, signs of shock, ischaemic chest discomfort, acute heart failure
    - Bradycardia with stable ventricular escape rhythms
  - <u>Precautions and considerations</u>:
    - Contraindicated in severe hypothermia
    - Conscious patients require analgesia for discomfort unless delay in sedation will cause or contribute to deterioration
    - Carotid pulse is not useful for confirming mechanical capture as muscular jerking after electrical stimulation may mimic the carotid pulse
  - <u>Sedation for pacing</u> - most conscious patient should be sedated before pacing unless rapidly deteiriorating:
    - Give parenteral narcotic for analgesia
    - Give parenteral benzodiazepine for anxiety and muscle contraction
  - <u>Technique</u>:
    - 1\. Place electrodes on chest according to package instruction
    - 2\. Turn the pacer on
    - 3\. Set demand rate to **60-80 bpm**, adjustable subsequently based on patient's clinical response once pacing is established
    - 4\. Set current as **2mA above dose at which consistent capture is observed** (on 12-lead ECG) 
    ![](img/electrical_capture_of_tcp_on_ecg.png)
- **Standby pacing in patients with ACS** - clinically stable ACS patients may decompensate suddenly due to bradyarrhythmias, initiate pacing when:
  - Symptomatic sinus node dysfunction with severe and symptomatic sinus bradycardia
  - Asymptomatic Mobitz type II second-degree AV block
  - Asymptomatic 3rd-degree AV block
  - Newly acquired left, right or alternating BBB or bifascicular block in setting of AMI

## Tachycardia: Stable and Unstable

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Tachycardia</u> - an arrhythmia with a heart rate \>= 100/min (takes on clinical significance if HR \>= 150 bpm)
  - <u>Symptomatic tachycardia</u> - S/S due to the rapid heart rate (unlikely causing haemodynamically instability if \< 150 bpm unless patient has ventricular dysfunction)
- **Rhythms for unstable tachycardia:**
  - Sinus tachycardia
  - Atrial fibrillation
  - Atrial flutter
  - Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)
  - Monomorphic VT
  - Polymorphic VT
  - Wide-complex tachycardia of uncertain type
- **Pathophysiology of unstable tachycardia:**
  - <u>Excessively fast heart rate</u> - shortened time for diastolic filling of ventricles resulting in overall reduction in cardiac output
  - <u>Ineffective contraction</u> - coordination between atria and ventricles or the ventricles themselves reduce cardiac output
- **Clinical features of unstable tachycardia:**
  - <u>Symptoms</u> - acute altered mental status, ischaemic chest discomfort, SOB
  - <u>Signs</u> - Hypotension, signs of shock (hypoperfusion), acute heart failure
- **Cardioversion:**
  - **Indications for immediate cardioversion:**
- **Adult tachycardia with a pulse algorithm** - steps for assessing the Mx of patient presenting w/ unstable tachycardia w/ a pulse: 
![](img/acls_adult_tachycardia_algorithm.png)
  - **Assessment of clinical condition** - appropriate sinus tachycardia can be seen in critically ill patients, however HR \> 150 bpm typically indicates tachyarrhythmia
  - **Identification and Tx of reversible causes** - O2, IV access, cardiac monitoring:
    - <u>Give O2</u> - correct hypoxaemia as a potential cause tachycardia
    - <u>IV access</u> - establish IV access for medications
    - <u>Establish monitoring</u> - cardiac monitoring (BP/P, rhythm), pulse oximetry (SpO2), +/- 12-lead ECG if available
    - <u>Primary assessment</u>:
      - **Airway** - maintain patent airway (check if patient can speak in words)
      - **Breathing** - monitor SpO2, give O2 if hypoxia, assist breathing as necessary
      - **Circulation** - cardiac monitor for BP/P, SpO2; obtain and review 12-lead ECG; establish IV access
      - **Disability and exposure** - focused P/E to look for reversible causes (e.g. hypoxia, toxicology causes)
    - **Assess stability** - assess the patient's degree of instability and determine if it is related to tachycardia, guiding subsequent steps taken:
      - **Unstable tachycardia:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - rate-related cardiovascular compromise with serious S/S (usually rate \> 150 bpm unless significant CVD)
        - <u>Subsequent Mx</u> - immediate synchronized cardioversion
      - **Stable tachycardia:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - absence of rate-related cardiovascular compromise
        - <u>Subsequent Mx</u> - obtain 12-lead ECG to determine rhythm, QRS width, and determine treatment options
  - **Immediate synchronized cardioversion** - for all unstable tachycardia:
    - **Indications for immediate synchronized cardioverion** - recommended for all unstable patients w/ pulse and following rhythms:
      - Unstable SVT
      - Unstable AF
      - Unstable AFlu
      - Unstable regular monomorphic tachycardia
    - **Indications for unsynchronised shock** - only considered if:
      - Polymorphic VT
      - Patients w/o a pulse (VF/ pVT)
      - Pre-arrest
    - **Pre-medication** - effective regimens include sedative +/- analgesic or anaesthetic agent if available:
      - <u>Sedatives</u> - diazapem, midazolam, etomidate, methohexital, propofol
      - <u>Analgesic</u> - fentanyl, morphine
    - **Steps for synchronised cardioversion:**
      - 1\. Pre-mediacate all conscious patients unless unstable or rapidly deteriorating
      - 2\. Turn on defibrillator (monophasic or biphasic)
      - 3\. Attach monitor leads to patient and ensure proper display of the patients rhythm (position adhesive electrode pads on patient)
      - 4\. Engage synchronisation mode
      - 5.Look for markers on R wave indicating sync mode
      - 6\. Adjust monitor gain if necessary until sync markers occur with each R wave
      - 7\. Select appropriate energy level according to device recommendations to maximize success of first shock
      - 8\. Announce to team members "Chargiing defibrillator - stand clear!"
      - 9\. Press the shock button
      - 10\. Check monitor - if tachycardia persist increase the energy level based on device recommendations (resynchronisation might be necessary as most defibrillators defaults to unsynchronised modes to enable immediate shock if produces VF)
    - **Consider unsynchronised shocks** - if condition critical
  - **Approach to stable tachycardia** - if absence of rate-related cardiac compromise:
    - <u>Obtain 12-lead ECG</u> - assessment of regularity and QRS complex to better define the rhythm
    - <u>Determine Mx pathway</u>:
      - **Wide complex tachycardia** - adenosine only if regular and mono-morphic, antiarrhythmic infusion for stable WCT
      - **Regular narrow complex tachycardia** - vagal maneuvers, adenosine, rate control with BBs or CCBs
      - **Irregular narrow complex tachycardia** - rate control with BBs or CCBs

